      "contentType": "text",
      "media": [],
      "hasMediaInOriginal": false,
      "fallbackReason": null,
      "text": "⚡🇪🇺 Kaja Kallas, the EU’s chief diplomat, may gain enhanced powers to coordinate the European Commission’s external policy units, including migration and development aid, under proposals from Paris and Berlin. \n\nThese efforts aim to reform the European External Action Service (EEAS) amid power politics, AI, and budget constraints. The EEAS has faced internal conflicts with Ursula von der Leyen’s Commission, with some suggesting it be fully integrated under the Commission. \n\nThe Franco-German proposal seeks to strengthen Kallas’s role while acknowledging the Commission’s foreign policy authority. It also proposes transferring some EEAS staff to the Commission to reduce the EU budget. The proposal will be discussed by EU foreign ministers in Ireland, though major reforms are unlikely before 2029, with Kallas gathering feedback from capitals on strategic foreign policy alignment.\n\nThe five “guiding questions” seek feedback on how: \n\n- The EU can become a more “strategically assertive global foreign actor”.\n\n- The EEAS can get more value from its 145 delegations.\n\n- It can deliver on its treaty mandate.\n\n- It can streamline its institutional setup with the Commission and Council.\n\n- It can contribute more effectively to leaders’ discussions.\n\n@MedmanNews",
      "links": [
        {
          "label": "@MedmanNews",
          "url": "https://t.me/MedmanNews"
        }
      ],
